Room has an Entrance. Slime — place a Slime (Status) marker on your Character board. Fire — place a Fire marker in this Room. Malfunction — place a Malfunction marker in this Room. Doors — place a Door token in the Corridor by which you entered.” “After resolving an Exploration token, remove it from the game into the box; it does not return to a supply or stay on the Room (each is resolved only once, on first entry).” 3. “A Silence or Danger Exploration result replaces the Movement Noise roll: resolve that result and do not roll Noise again afterward.” “Otherwise, perform the ordinary Noise roll if the destination is empty (no Characters, no Intruders).” 4. “If anyone — Character or Intruder — is already there, do NOT roll for Movement Noise.”"},
{"id":"Q06","requested_parts":["Careful Movement's Action-card cost and what it replaces.","When Careful Movement is forbidden.","Whether a Closed Door can be crossed and whether a miniature may stop in a Corridor."],"answer":"1. Careful Movement costs 2 Action cards. Instead of making a Noise roll after moving, place a Noise marker in a chosen Corridor connected to the destination Room; it also replaces automatic Silence/Danger Exploration results with that chosen placement. 2. It cannot be used in Combat or when all Corridors connected to the destination already have Noise markers. 3. No: Closed Doors block passage under normal Movement rules, which Careful Movement follows. A miniature cannot stop in any Corridor.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Careful Movement (cost 2) — follows normal Movement restrictions, including Closed Doors blocking passage; but instead of a Noise roll after moving, place a Noise marker in a chosen Corridor connected to the Room you enter. You cannot Carefully Move if all Corridors connected to the destination already hold Noise markers, and you cannot use it while in Combat. The FAQ clarifies that Careful Movement also replaces automatic Silence/Danger Exploration results with its chosen Noise placement; Reconnaissance cancels those automatic results too.” 3. “Characters and Intruders may only occupy Rooms (Special Rooms — Hibernatorium, Cockpit, Engines — count as Rooms; Escape Pod tokens are the only exception for Characters). Corridors connect Rooms; no one may ever stop in a Corridor.” “Two Rooms are neighboring when directly connected by 1 Corridor. A Closed Door blocks the \\"neighboring\\" status for players' Movement.”"},
{"id":"Q07","requested_parts":["What occurs when a Noise marker would be placed in a Corridor that already has one, including Technical Corridors.","The Encounter's Noise-removal procedure.","The drawn token's placement/return handling.","The Surprise Attack comparison and whether Contamination cards count."],"answer":"1. A Corridor may hold no more than 1 Noise marker; trying to place one where one already exists resolves an Encounter. A marker in the shared Technical Corridors space counts as present at every Technical Corridors Entrance on the board. 2. At the start of an Encounter, discard all Noise markers from all Corridors connected to the Room, including the Technical Corridors space if there is an Entrance. 3. Draw an Intruder token. If it is non-Blank, keep it aside outside the bag and place its matching Intruder miniature in the Room; it is not immediately returned. 4. Compare that token's number with the triggering player's total hand cards, counting both Action and Contamination cards. Fewer cards than the token's number means a Surprise Attack against the triggering Character; equal or more means no Surprise Attack.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Result 1, 2, 3, or 4: place a Noise marker in the Corridor with that number connected to the Room you entered (counting Technical Corridors if the Room has an Entrance). A Corridor may never hold more than 1 Noise marker — if instructed to place a marker where one already is, resolve an Encounter instead.” “If a Noise marker's number leads to a Technical Corridors Entrance, place the marker on the Technical Corridors space. A Noise marker there is treated as if it were in every Technical Corridors Entrance on the whole board. The 1-marker limit applies: placing a second one there resolves an Encounter instead.” 2. “Resolve: 1. Discard ALL Noise markers from all Corridors connected to the Room (including Technical Corridors if there's an Entrance).” 3. “Draw 1 Intruder token from the bag (symbol on one side, number on the other). Keep a non-Blank token aside outside the bag; it is not immediately returned. A Blank follows its separate return procedure below. 3. Place the matching Intruder miniature in the Room.” [The summary labels placement as step 3; quotation includes the operative sentence.] 4. “Compare the token's number to the number of cards in the triggering player's hand (Action AND Contamination cards both count). Fewer cards than the number → Surprise Attack (an Intruder Attack against that Character). Equal or more → nothing happens.”"},
{"id":"Q08","requested_parts":["How Danger resolves when several eligible neighboring Intruders exist and what happens if none are eligible.","Whether Intruders moving into the Character's Room count as an Encounter.","How Slime affects Silence."],"answer":"1. Move all eligible Intruders from neighboring Rooms into the Room; eligibility means not in Combat with any Character. If none are eligible, put a Noise marker in each connected Corridor that lacks one, including the Technical Corridors when applicable. 2. No. An Intruder merely moving into a Room with a Character is not an Encounter; it puts them in Combat. 3. With Slime on the Character board, a Silence result is resolved as Danger.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Danger — no Movement Noise roll. Move ALL eligible Intruders from neighboring Rooms into this Room (eligible means not in Combat with any Character). If there are none eligible, place a Noise marker in every Corridor connected to this Room that lacks one, counting Technical Corridors if the Room has an Entrance.” 2. “An Intruder merely moving into a Room with a Character is NOT an Encounter (they are simply in Combat).” 3. “Silence — no Movement Noise roll. Nothing happens. If your Character has a Slime marker, resolve \\"Danger\\" instead.”"},
{"id":"Q09","requested_parts":["What happens to connected Noise and the Blank token during an Encounter.","When the Encounter adds an Adult to the bag.","How Blank works during Intruder bag development."],"answer":"1. In a Blank Encounter, place a Noise marker in each Corridor connected to the Room and return the Blank token to the bag; no Intruder miniature appears. 2. Add 1 Adult token to the bag only if the Blank was the last token in the bag, and only if an Adult token is available. 3. In bag development, add 1 Adult token if one is available (otherwise nothing happens), then return the Blank token to the bag.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Blank token in an Encounter: place a Noise marker in each Corridor connected to that Room. If the Blank was the LAST token in the bag: add 1 Adult token to the bag (if any are available; otherwise nothing). Return the Blank token to the bag. The Encounter ends (no miniature appears).” 2. The quoted Encounter rule in part 1 expressly conditions the Adult addition on the Blank being the last token in the bag and availability of an Adult. 3. “Blank — add 1 Adult Intruder token to the bag (if none are available, nothing happens). Return the Blank token to the bag.”"},
{"id":"Q10","requested_parts":["Where both Intruders end and what happens to the Closed Door when they move through it.","Whether the resulting Destroyed Door can later be closed.","Whether a Closed Door blocks a Noise-triggered Intruder appearance through that Corridor."],"answer":"1. Both Intruders stay in their original Room, and the Closed Door is destroyed (token lying down). When multiple Intruders attempt the same closed Corridor together, they destroy the Door simultaneously and all stay put. 2. Only the Plasma Torch Item can later close a Destroyed Door. 3. No. If a Noise roll causes an Intruder to come through a Corridor with a Closed Door, it appears regardless of the Door.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Closed = standing token: blocks Character and Intruder movement (and Grenade throws). An Intruder trying to move through a Closed Door does not move — it destroys the Door instead (several Intruders moving from the same Room destroy it simultaneously and all stay put). Destroyed = lying token: movement allowed — only the Plasma Torch Item can Close a Destroyed Door.” 2. “Destroyed = lying token: movement allowed — only the Plasma Torch Item can Close a Destroyed Door. Other Door-closing effects cannot Close it.” 3. “If a Noise roll causes an Intruder to come through a Corridor with a Closed Door, it appears regardless of the Door.”"}
]
}`;
