{"id":"Q04","requested_parts":["Timing of the Fire wound after one Action and passing.","Whether the Character may take more normal Actions this Player Phase.","Whether they may discard hand cards when passing.","Whether later players' rounds cause them more Fire wounds."],"answer":"1. Taking only one Action means the Character must pass; they take 1 Light Wound at the end of their own passing round. 2. They cannot take further normal Actions in this Player Phase (Interruption remains permitted). 3. At the moment of passing they may discard any number of hand cards. 4. No; later players' rounds cause no additional Fire wound to them that turn.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “If you perform only 1 Action instead of two, you must pass.” “A Character who ends their round in a Room with a Fire marker suffers 1 Light Wound (every round; only after finishing their own round — passing players take it once at the end of their round and no more that turn).” 2. “Normal Actions | You cannot take further normal Actions; Interruption remains permitted as below.” 3. “Hand cards | At the moment you pass, you may discard any number of cards from your hand.” 4. “Fire in your Room | Take 1 Light Wound at the end of your own passing round. Later players' rounds do not give you further Fire wounds that turn.”"},
{"id":"Q05","requested_parts":["Reveal of the unexplored Room and setting its Item Counter.","Resolution and destination of the Exploration token.","When Movement Noise is rolled, including Silence/Danger.","Effect of an occupied destination on Movement Noise."],"answer":"1. Flip the unexplored tile face up. For a normal Room tile, orient the Exploration token's number to the board's Item Counter symbol; the Nest and Room Covered With Slime have no Item count. 2. Resolve the token effect (Silence, Danger, Slime, Fire, Malfunction, or Doors; Doors go in the Corridor used to enter), then remove the token from the game into the box. 3. Silence or Danger replaces the Movement Noise roll; otherwise roll only if the destination is empty. 4. If a Character or Intruder is already there, do not roll Movement Noise.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “After entering a Room: (1) if the Room tile was unexplored (face down), flip it face up and reveal and resolve its Exploration token, then return that token to the box (removed from the game); (2) apply the Movement Noise rule.” “Item Counter: the Nest and Room Covered With Slime get no Item count. For other Room tiles, rotate the tile until the Exploration token's number faces the Item Counter symbol printed on the board next to the slot.” 2. “Resolve the effect: Silence — no Movement Noise roll. Nothing happens. If your Character has a Slime marker, resolve ‘Danger’ instead. Danger — no Movement Noise roll. Move ALL eligible Intruders from neighboring Rooms into this Room (eligible means not in Combat with any Character). If there are none eligible, place a Noise marker in every Corridor connected to this Room that lacks one, counting Technical Corridors if the Room has an Entrance. Slime — place a Slime (Status) marker on your Character board. Fire — place a Fire marker in this Room. Malfunction — place a Malfunction marker in this Room. Doors — place a Door token in the Corridor by which you entered.” “After resolving an Exploration token, remove it from the game into the box; it does not return to a supply or stay on the Room (each is resolved only once, on first entry).” 3. “A Silence or Danger Exploration result replaces the Movement Noise roll: resolve that result and do not roll Noise again afterward.” “Otherwise, perform the ordinary Noise roll if the destination is empty (no Characters, no Intruders).” 4. “If anyone — Character or Intruder — is already there, do NOT roll for Movement Noise.”"},
{"id":"Q06","requested_parts":["Careful Movement's cost and what it replaces.","When Careful Movement is forbidden.","Closed Door and stopping-in-Corridor restrictions."],"answer":"1. It costs 2 Action cards, replaces the after-movement Noise roll with placing a Noise marker in a chosen Corridor connected to the destination, and also replaces automatic Silence/Danger Exploration results with that placement. 2. It is forbidden in Combat and if all Corridors connected to the destination already have Noise markers. 3. It follows normal movement restrictions, so a Closed Door blocks passage; miniatures may never stop in Corridors.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Careful Movement (cost 2) — follows normal Movement restrictions, including Closed Doors blocking passage; but instead of a Noise roll after moving, place a Noise marker in a chosen Corridor connected to the Room you enter. You cannot Carefully Move if all Corridors connected to the destination already hold Noise markers, and you cannot use it while in Combat. The FAQ clarifies that Careful Movement also replaces automatic Silence/Danger Exploration results with its chosen Noise placement; Reconnaissance cancels those automatic results too.” 3. “Characters and Intruders may only occupy Rooms (Special Rooms — Hibernatorium, Cockpit, Engines — count as Rooms; Escape Pod tokens are the only exception for Characters). Corridors connect Rooms; no one may ever stop in a Corridor.” “Two Rooms are neighboring when directly connected by 1 Corridor. A Closed Door blocks the ‘neighboring’ status for players' Movement.”"},
{"id":"Q07","requested_parts":["Effect of trying to place Noise where a marker already exists, including Technical Corridors.","Noise removal at Encounter start.","Drawn token placement and return handling.","Surprise Attack comparison and whether Contamination counts."],"answer":"1. A Corridor holds at most 1 Noise marker; attempting to place another resolves an Encounter. A marker on the shared Technical Corridors space counts for every Technical Corridors Entrance. 2. Discard all Noise markers from all Corridors connected to the Encounter Room, including Technical Corridors if there is an Entrance. 3. Draw an Intruder token; keep a non-Blank token aside outside the bag (not immediately returned) and place its matching miniature in the Room. 4. Compare the token number to all cards in the triggering player's hand, counting Action and Contamination cards. Fewer cards than the number causes a Surprise Attack against that Character; equal or more causes no attack.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Result 1, 2, 3, or 4: place a Noise marker in the Corridor with that number connected to the Room you entered (counting Technical Corridors if the Room has an Entrance). A Corridor may never hold more than 1 Noise marker — if instructed to place a marker where one already is, resolve an Encounter instead.” “If a Noise marker's number leads to a Technical Corridors Entrance, place the marker on the Technical Corridors space. A Noise marker there is treated as if it were in every Technical Corridors Entrance on the whole board. The 1-marker limit applies: placing a second one there resolves an Encounter instead.” 2. “Resolve: 1. Discard ALL Noise markers from all Corridors connected to the Room (including Technical Corridors if there's an Entrance).” 3. “Draw 1 Intruder token from the bag (symbol on one side, number on the other). Keep a non-Blank token aside outside the bag; it is not immediately returned. A Blank follows its separate return procedure below.” “Place the matching Intruder miniature in the Room.” 4. “Compare the token's number to the number of cards in the triggering player's hand (Action AND Contamination cards both count). Fewer cards than the number → Surprise Attack (an Intruder Attack against that Character). Equal or more → nothing happens.”"},
{"id":"Q08","requested_parts":["Danger with eligible neighboring Intruders and with none eligible.","Whether moving into a Character's Room is an Encounter.","Slime's effect on Silence."],"answer":"1. Move all eligible Intruders from neighboring Rooms into the Room; an eligible Intruder is not in Combat with any Character. If none are eligible, place Noise in each connected Corridor that lacks a marker, including Technical Corridors when applicable. 2. No: merely moving into a Room with a Character is not an Encounter; they are simply in Combat. 3. Slime makes Silence resolve as Danger.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Danger — no Movement Noise roll. Move ALL eligible Intruders from neighboring Rooms into this Room (eligible means not in Combat with any Character). If there are none eligible, place a Noise marker in every Corridor connected to this Room that lacks one, counting Technical Corridors if the Room has an Entrance.” 2. “An Intruder merely moving into a Room with a Character is NOT an Encounter (they are simply in Combat).” 3. “Silence — no Movement Noise roll. Nothing happens. If your Character has a Slime marker, resolve ‘Danger’ instead.”"},
{"id":"Q09","requested_parts":["Noise and Blank handling during an Encounter.","When an Adult is added during a Blank Encounter.","Blank handling during bag development."],"answer":"1. Place a Noise marker in each Corridor connected to the Room; return the Blank token to the bag, and no miniature appears. 2. Add 1 Adult token only if the Blank was the last token in the bag and an Adult is available. 3. In bag development, add an Adult if one is available (otherwise nothing happens), then return Blank to the bag.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Blank token in an Encounter: place a Noise marker in each Corridor connected to that Room. If the Blank was the LAST token in the bag: add 1 Adult token to the bag (if any are available; otherwise nothing). Return the Blank token to the bag. The Encounter ends (no miniature appears).” 2. The quoted rule expressly makes this addition conditional on the Blank being the last token in the bag and an Adult being available. 3. “Blank — add 1 Adult Intruder token to the bag (if none are available, nothing happens). Return the Blank token to the bag.”"},
{"id":"Q10","requested_parts":["Where the Intruders end and what happens to the Closed Door.","Whether a Destroyed Door can be closed later.","Whether a Closed Door blocks a Noise-triggered Intruder appearance."],"answer":"1. Both Intruders stay in their original Room, and the Door is destroyed. When several Intruders move from that Room through the same Closed Door, they destroy it simultaneously and all stay put. 2. Only the Plasma Torch can close a Destroyed Door. 3. No; an Intruder arriving through that Corridor due to a Noise roll appears regardless of the Closed Door.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Closed = standing token: blocks Character and Intruder movement (and Grenade throws). An Intruder trying to move through a Closed Door does not move — it destroys the Door instead (several Intruders moving from the same Room destroy it simultaneously and all stay put). Destroyed = lying token: movement allowed — only the Plasma Torch Item can Close a Destroyed Door.” 2. “Destroyed = lying token: movement allowed — only the Plasma Torch Item can Close a Destroyed Door. Other Door-closing effects cannot Close it.” 3. “If a Noise roll causes an Intruder to come through a Corridor with a Closed Door, it appears regardless of the Door.”"}
]
}`;
