const raw = String.raw`[
{"id":"Q21","requested_parts":["Placement of basic and additional Room tiles and Exploration tokens.","Starting Coordinates, Destination marker, and Time marker.","Starting Engine tokens and arrangement.","Starting Eggs and Weakness cards."],"answer":"1. Shuffle Room tiles 2 and place one face down in each slot marked 2, returning extras unseen; shuffle and place all 11 Room tiles 1 face down on their slots. Shuffle Exploration tokens face down and put one on each tile; return extras to the box. 2. Put 1 random Coordinates card face down by the Cockpit, set the Destination marker to B, and set the Time marker on the green Time Track space. 3. For each of Engines 1–3, shuffle one Damaged and one Working token face down and stack them face down on that Engine's slot; the top token is the true status and must not be seen. 4. Put 5 Egg tokens and 3 random Weakness cards face down in their Intruder-board slots.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Shuffle all Room tiles ‘2’ without looking at their fronts and randomly place one face down on each Room slot marked ‘2’. Return the rest to the box unseen (there are more tiles than slots — players never know exactly which Rooms are on the ship).” “Shuffle all 11 Room tiles ‘1’ without looking at their fronts and randomly place one face down on each slot marked ‘1’. All 11 are used.” “Shuffle the Exploration tokens (unseen) and place one face down on each Room tile. Return the rest to the box.” 2. “Place 1 random Coordinates card face down on its space next to the Cockpit. Return the rest to the box.” “Place 1 Status marker on the ‘B’ space of the Destination Track — this is the Destination marker.” “Place 1 Status marker on the green space of the Time Track — this is the Time marker.” 3. “For each Engine (1, 2, 3): take its two Engine tokens (1 Damaged, 1 Working), shuffle them face down, and stack them face down on the matching Engine slot. The top token indicates the true status of that Engine. Players must not see the token fronts.” 4. “Place the Intruder board next to the board with 5 Egg tokens and 3 random Weakness cards face down in their slots. Return remaining Weakness cards to the box.”"},
{"id":"Q22","requested_parts":["How Player Numbers are assigned and how Character drafting proceeds.","What each Character receives and where the miniature begins.","Where the initial Character Corpse begins.","Whether Quest Items are immediately usable."],"answer":"1. Randomly deal Help cards numbered 1 through the player count; each player's number determines draft order. Shuffle Character draft cards; in number order each player draws 2, reveals both publicly, chooses 1, then shuffles the unchosen card back before the next player draws. 2. Keep the Help card and matching numbered Inventory; take the Character board, miniature in a colored ring in the Hibernatorium, shuffled 10-card Action deck, Starting Item Weapon in a Hand slot loaded to its Ammo capacity, and 2 Quest Items horizontal next to the board. 3. Put the blue Character Corpse token in the Hibernatorium. 4. Quest Items are not active at the start; they unlock by completing mini-quests.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Randomly deal Player Numbers: use Help cards numbered 1 to the player count and deal one at random to each player. This is their Player Number, which sets character-choice order and matters for some Objectives.” “Character draft, in Player Number order: shuffle the Character draft cards. Each player draws 2 → reveals both publicly → chooses 1 → shuffles the unchosen card back into the draft deck before the next player draws.” 2. “Complete starting kit: keep your Help card and matching numbered Inventory holder from steps 14–15, and take: (A) your Character board; (B) your miniature (in a colored plastic ring), placed in the Hibernatorium; (C) your 10-card Action deck, shuffled, face down at the left of your board; (D) your Starting Item card (Weapon) in one of your two Hand slots, loaded with Ammo markers equal to that Weapon's Ammo capacity; (E) your 2 Quest Items, horizontal side up, next to the board (NOT active at the start — they unlock via mini-quests).” 3. “Place the blue Character Corpse token in the Hibernatorium — a dead crew member. During the game treat it as a Character Corpse Object; the blue token is always assigned to the first dead body found on the ship.” 4. The complete starting-kit sentence in part 2 explicitly says the Quest Items are “NOT active at the start — they unlock via mini-quests.”"},
{"id":"Q23","requested_parts":["When Action hands are refilled and the First Player token is passed.","What happens when a required draw finds an empty Action deck.","Normal discard destinations and visibility."],"answer":"1. At the start of the Player Phase, every player draws up to 5 Action cards. After drawing, pass the First Player token left, except on the first game turn. 2. If a required draw finds the Action deck empty, shuffle that Character's discard pile into a new Action deck, then draw. 3. Normally, discarded markers return to their matching supply pool. Cards are placed face up in their appropriate discard piles: used Action and gained Contamination cards go to that Character's Action discard pile; Items to their matching Item discard; Event, Intruder Attack, and Serious Wound cards to their corresponding discard piles.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Draw Action cards — every player draws from their Action deck up to a hand of 5 cards. Whenever you must draw and your Action deck is empty, shuffle your discard pile into a new Action deck, then draw.” “First Player token — after drawing, pass the token to the player on the left (do NOT pass it on the first turn of the game).” 2. The same draw rule states: “Whenever you must draw and your Action deck is empty, shuffle your discard pile into a new Action deck, then draw.” 3. “Normal discard destinations include marker pools and card piles: return a discarded marker to its matching supply pool, and place a discarded card face up in the appropriate pile listed below.” The table specifies: “Used/discarded Action cards | That Character's Action discard pile”; “Gained Contamination cards | On top of that Character's Action discard pile; never paid as Action costs”; “Discarded Item cards | Matching Item discard pile”; “Discarded Event cards | Event discard pile”; “Discarded Intruder Attack cards | Intruder Attack discard pile”; “Discarded Serious Wound cards | Serious Wound discard pile”; “Discarded markers | Return to the appropriate marker pool.”"},
{"id":"Q24","requested_parts":["Complete Event Phase sequence.","Which Intruders move on an Event card and how destination/corridor are chosen.","When the Event effect resolves.","What happens when the Event deck or Intruder Attack deck runs out."],"answer":"1. Advance Time and Self-Destruct if active, resolve Intruder Attacks, resolve Fire Damage, resolve the Event card (movement then effect), then develop the Intruder bag. 2. Move each Intruder showing the Event card's symbol only if it is not in a Room with any Character; move it to a neighboring Room through the Corridor matching the card's number. If that number indicates a Technical Corridor, remove its miniature and return its token to the Intruder bag. 3. Resolve the Event's effect after movement, then discard the card unless it says otherwise. 4. If the Event deck runs out, shuffle the Event discard pile into a new deck; if the Intruder Attack deck runs out, shuffle its discard pile into a new deck.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Complete order: advance Time/Self-Destruct → Intruder Attacks → Fire Damage → Event card (movement, then effect) → Intruder bag development.” 2. “Intruder movement: all Intruders showing the card's Intruder symbol that are NOT in a Room with any Characters move to a neighboring Room through the Corridor matching the number on the card. If the number indicates a Technical Corridor, remove that Intruder's miniature from the board and put its token back in the Intruder bag.” 3. “Event effect: after the movement step, resolve the effect described on the card.” “Then discard the Event card (unless the card says otherwise). If the Event deck ever runs out (rare), shuffle the Event discards into a new deck.” 4. The Event rule quoted in part 3 provides the Event-deck recycling rule. The Intruder Attack procedure says: “For other Intruders, draw 1 Intruder Attack card: if it shows a symbol matching the attacking Intruder's type, resolve the card's effect; otherwise the attack misses. Ignore the blood icon during attacks. Discard the card to the Intruder Attack discard pile; if the deck runs out, reshuffle the discard pile into a new deck.”"},
{"id":"Q25","requested_parts":["Bag-development outcome and token handling for Larva and Creeper.","Adult and Breeder outcome, including Noise rollers and return timing.","The two Queen outcomes and token return.","When the turn ends."],"answer":"1. Larva: remove the token from the bag and add 1 Adult token. Creeper: remove it and add 1 Breeder token. 2. Adult: return its token to the bag immediately before all players roll Noise in order, skipping Characters in Combat; an Encounter from a roll can draw that same Adult. Breeder: all players roll Noise in order, skipping those in Combat, then return the Breeder token. 3. Queen: if any Characters are in the Nest, place the Queen miniature there and resolve an Encounter; if no Character is in the Nest or it has not been discovered, add 1 Egg token to the Intruder board. Return the Queen token to the bag. 4. The turn ends only after all bag-development consequences, including Encounters and Surprise Attacks, have been resolved; then a new turn starts.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Larva — remove this token from the bag; add 1 Adult token to the bag.” “Creeper — remove this token from the bag; add 1 Breeder token to the bag.” 2. “Adult — return the token to the bag immediately before all players roll for Noise in order (skip Characters in Combat). An Encounter from those rolls may draw that same Adult token (FAQ Rules 7).” “Breeder — all players roll for Noise in order (skip players in Combat), then return the Breeder token to the bag.” 3. “Queen — if any Characters are in the Nest Room, place the Queen miniature there and resolve an Encounter. If no Characters are in the Nest (or it hasn't been discovered), add 1 Egg token to the Intruder board. Return the token to the bag.” 4. “End of the turn — a new turn starts. The turn only ends once the bag development step is fully resolved (all Encounters, Surprise Attacks, etc. played out).”"},
{"id":"Q26","requested_parts":["What Fire and Malfunction marker-pool exhaustion does.","What happens if the target Room already has the corresponding marker.","Where discarded markers go.","Who dies when hull integrity is lost."],"answer":"1. There are 8 Fire markers and 8 Malfunction markers; an instruction to place a ninth Fire marker explodes the ship and ends the game, while a ninth Malfunction marker causes hull-integrity loss and ends the game. 2. Only 1 of either marker may be in a Room; additional placements when one is already there do nothing. 3. Discarded markers return to their matching supply pool. 4. Hull loss destroys the ship: all Characters aboard, awake or hibernated, and everything else aboard, including Intruders, die.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Fire markers: (1) a Character ending their round in a burning Room suffers 1 Light Wound (see Turn Structure); (2) each Event Phase, each Intruder in a burning Room suffers 1 Injury and immediately makes its usual Injury Effect check; (3) there are only 8 Fire markers — if instructed to place one when the pool is empty, the ship explodes and the game ends.” “Malfunction markers: (1) the Room's Action is unavailable (Searching still works); repair effects (Repair Action cards, Tools Item) can discard the marker; (2) only 8 exist — if instructed to place one when the pool is empty, the ship loses hull integrity and the game ends.” 2. Fire: “Max 1 per Room (extra placements do nothing).” Malfunction: “Max 1 per Room: if instructed to place another Malfunction marker in a Room already holding one, nothing happens.” 3. “Normal discard destinations include marker pools and card piles: return a discarded marker to its matching supply pool, and place a discarded card face up in the appropriate pile.” 4. “The Self-Destruct marker reaches the final red (‘skull’) space of its Track, OR you are instructed to place a 9th Fire marker, OR a 9th Malfunction marker — the ship explodes / loses hull integrity: ALL Characters on board (awake and hibernated) die, and everything else aboard dies too, including Intruders (relevant to some Objectives).”"},
{"id":"Q27","requested_parts":["What a Malfunction disables and what remains usable.","Where Malfunction markers can never be placed.","Whether Malfunction changes Engine status or affects an already-hibernating Character."],"answer":"1. A Malfunction disables the Room's Action, but Searching remains usable; it also makes a Room's Computer unavailable. 2. It is never placed in the Nest or Room Covered With Slime, and cannot be placed in unexplored Rooms. 3. It does not change the Engine token status; it affects only that Room's Action. A Hibernatorium Malfunction does not affect already-hibernated Characters.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Malfunction markers: (1) the Room's Action is unavailable (Searching still works); repair effects (Repair Action cards, Tools Item) can discard the marker.” “A Malfunction makes a Room's Computer unavailable.” 2. “Never placed in the Nest or the Room Covered In Slime.” “Malfunction markers cannot be placed in unexplored Rooms, whereas Fire markers can (FAQ Rules 22).” 3. “They disable Special Rooms too (a Malfunction in the Hibernatorium doesn't affect already-hibernated Characters; Engine status is never affected, only the Room Action).”"},
{"id":"Q28","requested_parts":["Which Items may be hidden, what others see, Inventory limit, and visibility when an Item is used.","How Heavy Items/Objects occupy Hand slots.","How attached/add Items and Duct Tape work."],"answer":"1. Normal Items are hidden in the card holder; other players see only backs/colors. There is no Inventory size limit. Any Item used must be shown to all players. 2. Heavy Items and Objects are never hidden and each occupies one of 2 Hand slots; a third requires dropping one first. 3. Add-type Items attach to another card and act as one. Duct Tape combines 2 Heavy Item cards into one unit occupying 1 Hand slot, but it cannot combine Heavy Objects.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Inventory (normal Items): hidden in your card holder — others see only card backs/colors. No Inventory size limit. Any Item used must be shown to all players.” 2. “Heavy Items and Objects (Hand icon): never hidden; each occupies one of your 2 Hand slots. To take a third, Drop one first.” 3. “‘Add’-type Items (Extended Magazine, Autoloader) attach to another card and act as one. Duct Tape combines 2 Heavy Item cards into one unit occupying 1 Hand slot; it cannot combine Heavy Objects.”"},
{"id":"Q29","requested_parts":["When and at what Action cost Items and Objects can be dropped, and where each goes.","Ammo quantity on Weapons found by Searching versus Starting Weapons."],"answer":"1. Dropping is free, requires no Action, and may be done any time during your turn. Dropped Objects remain in the Room; dropped Items are lost and discarded. 2. A Weapon found by Searching starts with 1 Ammo, while a Starting Weapon is loaded to its Ammo capacity; a Weapon may never exceed its printed capacity.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Drop: free, any time during your turn, no Action. Dropped Objects stay in the Room; dropped Items are lost (discarded).” 2. “A Weapon found by Searching starts with 1 Ammo. This Ammo instruction applies to Weapons, rather than to every Heavy Item. A Weapon can never exceed its printed Ammo capacity. Starting and Crafted Weapons use their own loading rules.” The starting-kit rule specifies: “your Starting Item card (Weapon) in one of your two Hand slots, loaded with Ammo markers equal to that Weapon's Ammo capacity.”"},
{"id":"Q30","requested_parts":["How the Search deck is chosen and how cards are drawn, kept, and returned.","How Search changes the Item Counter and what happens at zero.","Search-prohibited Rooms and Blue Item availability."],"answer":"1. Search Red, Yellow, or Green according to the Room's color; in a white Room, choose any of those three decks. Draw 2 from the selected deck, keep 1, and return the other face down to the bottom of that deck without shuffling. 2. Each Search lowers the Item Counter by 1; at zero the Room cannot be searched, except for Scout's Scavenging. 3. Hibernatorium, Cockpit, and Engines 1–3 cannot be Searched; the Nest and Room Covered With Slime also prohibit any Search Action. Blue Items are Crafted and are never found by Searching.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Search procedure: Red (Military), Yellow (Technical) and Green (Medical) Items can be found via the Search Action card; Blue Items are Crafted and never found by Searching. Match the Room's color (background under its name); in a white Room, choose any of the 3 decks. Draw 2 cards from that one deck → keep 1 → return the other face down to the bottom of that deck, without shuffling (FAQ Actions 6).” 2. “In other Rooms, each Search lowers the Item Counter by 1; at 0 the Room cannot be searched (exception: Scout's Scavenging).” 3. “The five printed Special Rooms — Hibernatorium, Cockpit and Engines 1–3 — cannot be Searched. The Nest and Room Covered With Slime also prohibit any Search Action, although they are Room tiles rather than those five printed Special Rooms.”"}
]`;
