const raw = String.raw`[
{"id":"Q31","requested_parts":["Craft Action-card and component payment and destination of components.","Whether the Crafted result must be available and whether the Craft deck can be inspected.","Which Crafted Items exist and their matching component symbols."],"answer":"1. Crafting costs 1 Action card plus 2 Item cards showing the matching blue component icons; discard those components and take the Crafted card whose gray icons match. 2. The resulting card must be available; you may look through the Craft deck at any time. 3. Antidote = flame + medical cross; Taser = battery/lightning + screwdriver/tool; Flamethrower = flame + screwdriver/tool; Molotov Cocktail = flame + folded cloth/fabric.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Craft Item — pay 1 Action card and discard 2 Item cards showing the appropriate blue Craft (Component) icons to take the Crafted Item card showing those same icons in gray. A Crafted Item cannot be built if its card is not available, even with the right components.” “To Craft, pay 1 Action card plus 2 matching Item components; discard those Items and take the available Crafted Item whose gray icons match their blue icons.” 2. “you may look through the Craft deck at any time.” “If the Crafted card is unavailable, it cannot be built.” 3. “The four Crafted Items and their component symbols (manual page 23) are: Antidote = flame + medical cross; Taser = battery/lightning + screwdriver/tool; Flamethrower = flame + screwdriver/tool; Molotov Cocktail = flame + folded cloth/fabric. These are component symbols: discard two Items showing the matching blue icons, rather than assuming a particular Item name.”"},
{"id":"Q32","requested_parts":["Who pays for Trade and who may join.","Whether a Character can give something for nothing or exchange Ammo.","Whether a searched Heavy Item requires a separate Pick Up Action."],"answer":"1. Only the initiating Character spends the Trade Action; the trade can include all Characters in the Room, and any of them can exchange with each other if both sides agree. 2. You may give an Item or Object for nothing, but Characters cannot exchange Ammo. 3. No separate Pick Up Action is needed for a Heavy Item gained by Searching; Pick Up Heavy Object applies to Objects, not Heavy Items.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Trade — trade with all Characters in your Room. Players show each other any Item cards/Objects they wish; exchanges happen if both parties agree. Only the initiator spends the Action.” “Any Characters in the Room can trade with each other, including pairs who did not initiate/pay (Actions 1–2).” 2. “You may give an Item/Object for nothing. Characters cannot exchange Ammo.” 3. “Pick Up Heavy Object (cost 1 Action card) — pick up 1 Heavy Object in your Room (Character Corpse, Intruder Carcass, or Intruder Egg) into a Hand slot. This Basic pickup has no Noise-roll instruction; taking an Egg still on the Intruder board instead uses the Nest Room Action, costs 2 cards and then rolls Noise. (Heavy Items found by Searching do not require this Action — it applies to Objects only.)”"},
{"id":"Q33","requested_parts":["Which Object corresponds to each Weakness and where it must be for analysis.","Whether the Object is consumed and who benefits from the revealed Weakness.","Whether the researcher must personally perform research for a Discover Weakness Objective."],"answer":"1. In the Laboratory, Analyse an Object present there: Character Corpse, Intruder Carcass, or Egg; reveal its corresponding Weakness card face up and leave it on the Intruder board. The summary identifies the three Weakness categories as Corpse, Egg, and Carcass. 2. The Object is not consumed; the Weakness rule benefits all players. 3. No. A corresponding Object must be researched in the Laboratory by anyone before game end; you need not personally research it.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Laboratory — Analyse 1 Object (Character Corpse, Intruder Carcass, or Egg present in the Room): reveal the corresponding Weakness card face up and leave it on the Intruder board.” The key numbers identify “3 face-down Weakness cards (Corpse / Egg / Carcass).” 2. “Its rule benefits all players. The Object is not consumed.” 3. “Discover a Weakness — a corresponding Object must be researched in the Laboratory by anyone before game end.”"},
{"id":"Q34","requested_parts":["Common prerequisites and cost for a Room Action.","Whether the Computer icon independently provides an Action or benefit.","Whether Fire affects Room Actions or Searching."],"answer":"1. A Room Action costs 2 Action cards; your Character must be in the Room whose Action is used, out of Combat, and with no Malfunction there, unless an explicit special ability allows otherwise. 2. A Computer icon grants no independent Action or benefit; it matters only when another rule refers to a Computer, and Malfunction makes it unavailable. 3. Fire does not block Room Actions or Searching.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Most Rooms grant a specific Action (see Player Reference). Cost: 2 Action cards. Room Actions are local: your Character must be in the Room whose Action you use, unless an explicit special ability permits otherwise. You cannot perform a Room Action while in Combat or in a Room with a Malfunction marker.” 2. “Computer icon: this identifies a Computer in the Room but grants no independent Action or benefit. It matters only when another rule refers to a Computer (for example, an Action, Item or Event card). A Malfunction makes that Computer unavailable, as though its icon were absent.” 3. “Fire markers: ... Room Actions and Searching still work in burning Rooms.”"},
{"id":"Q35","requested_parts":["What the Armory recharges, by how much, and the limits."],"answer":"1. The Armory adds 2 Ammo to 1 of your Energy Weapons. It cannot recharge a Classic Weapon and cannot take a Weapon above its Ammo capacity. It is a Room Action and uses the common Room Action requirements and cost: 2 Action cards, in that Room, out of Combat, and without a Malfunction there.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Armory — add 2 Ammo to 1 of your Energy Weapons (never Classic Weapons; never above capacity).” The common rule states: “Most Rooms grant a specific Action (see Player Reference). Cost: 2 Action cards. Room Actions are local: your Character must be in the Room whose Action you use, unless an explicit special ability permits otherwise. You cannot perform a Room Action while in Combat or in a Room with a Malfunction marker.”"},
{"id":"Q36","requested_parts":["The Emergency Room's three mutually exclusive treatment choices.","Whether dressing removes a Serious Wound or only its effect.","Whether duplicate Serious Wound effects stack."],"answer":"1. Choose exactly one: Dress all your Serious Wounds; Heal 1 Dressed Serious Wound; or Heal all your Light Wounds. 2. Dressing flips a Serious Wound card face down so its effect is ignored, but it still counts toward the 3-wound limit; it does not remove the Wound. 3. Identical Serious Wound cards' effects do not stack.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Emergency Room — Dress ALL your Serious Wounds OR Heal 1 Dressed Serious Wound OR Heal all your Light Wounds.” 2. “Dress a Serious Wound: flip the card face down — its effect is ignored, but it still counts toward the 3-wound limit.” 3. “Serious Wounds: draw a Serious Wound card and place it by your board; its effect applies immediately. Duplicate Serious Wound cards are NOT cumulative (but are harder to get rid of).”"},
{"id":"Q37","requested_parts":["What the Send a Signal Action does and its use.","Whether the Comms Room can be absent in a normal game."],"answer":"1. Send a Signal places a Status marker on your Character board's Signal space; this is used only for Objectives. 2. No. A Comms Room is always aboard.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Comms Room — Send a Signal: place a Status marker on your board's Signal space (only used for Objectives).” 2. “Send the Signal — perform the Comms Room Action (a Comms Room is always aboard).”"},
{"id":"Q38","requested_parts":["What Fire Control does to a chosen Room and Intruders when Fire is present.","What it does when the Room has no Fire."],"answer":"1. Choose any 1 Room: discard its Fire marker, if present, and all Intruders there run away in random directions; draw 1 Event card per Intruder. 2. It still scatters all Intruders when there is no Fire in the chosen Room.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Fire Control System — choose any 1 Room: discard its Fire marker (if any) and ALL Intruders there run away in random directions (draw 1 Event card per Intruder).” 2. “Works even with no Fire, to scatter Intruders.”"},
{"id":"Q39","requested_parts":["How Self-Destruct starts, stops, restarts, and advances; restrictions when someone is hibernating.","What happens at the first yellow space and whether later yellow spaces unlock Pods again.","What happens at the final space and during a hyperspace jump while active.","Whether a Character may start it if another Character is already hibernating."],"answer":"1. A Character uses the Generator Room Action to start or stop Self-Destruct; starting places the marker on the first green space. It advances 1 space whenever the Time marker advances. Stopping removes the marker, and restarting begins again from green. A Character cannot initiate it while anyone is hibernating, though Coolant Leak can start it despite sleepers. 2. At the first yellow space, the sequence can no longer be aborted and all Pods unlock; they can be re-Locked, and later yellow spaces do not unlock them again. 3. At the final skull space the ship explodes. If a hyperspace jump occurs while Self-Destruct is active, the ship is still considered destroyed. 4. No; Character initiation is forbidden while anyone is hibernating, with the Coolant Leak Event exception.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Generator — initiate (or stop) the Self-Destruct sequence (Characters cannot start it while anyone is hibernating; Coolant Leak Event is an exception).” “Starting it places a Status marker on the first (green) space of the Self-Destruct Track; the marker advances 1 space every time the Time marker advances. Any Character stopping the sequence removes the marker (a new sequence restarts from green).” “Characters cannot start Self-Destruct while any Character is hibernating; the Coolant Leak Event can start it despite sleepers (FAQ Rules 14).” 2. “At the first yellow space: the sequence can no longer be aborted, and all Escape Pods unlock instantly. They can be re-Locked; subsequent yellow spaces do not unlock them again (FAQ Rules 25).” 3. “On the final (’skull’) space: the ship explodes.” “If a hyperspace jump happens while Self-Destruct is active, the ship is still considered destroyed.” 4. The Generator and hibernation rule in part 1 expressly prohibits Character starting while anyone is hibernating and states the Coolant Leak exception."},
{"id":"Q40","requested_parts":["Which Room Airlock Control can target and the relevant Door condition.","What happens immediately after the target is selected.","What cancels the procedure and when it completes, including the outcome."],"answer":"1. Choose any 1 OTHER Yellow Room whose connected Corridors contain no Destroyed Doors. 2. Automatically close every Door around it and place the Airlock Procedure token there. 3. If any of those Doors is opened before the current Player Phase ends, the procedure is cancelled. If all remain Closed when that phase ends (all players have passed), everything in the Room dies, Characters and Intruders, and any Fire marker there is removed.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Airlock Control — pick any 1 OTHER Yellow Room whose connected Corridors contain no Destroyed Doors.” 2. “Automatically Close every Door around it and place the Airlock Procedure token there.” 3. “If all those Doors are still Closed when the current Player Phase ends (all players passed), EVERYTHING in that Room dies (Characters and Intruders) and any Fire marker there is removed; if any of the Doors is opened first, the procedure is cancelled.”"}
]`;
