const raw = String.raw`[
{"id":"Q41","requested_parts":["When Cabins increases the hand and to what size.","What disables the benefit and whether it is a paid Room Action."],"answer":"1. At the start of a new turn, a Character in Cabins with no Intruders present draws 1 extra Action card, for a hand of 6 instead of 5. 2. A Malfunction disables it. It is a passive effect, not a paid Room Action.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Cabins — passive: at the start of a new turn, a Character here with no Intruders present draws 1 extra Action card (hand of 6 instead of 5).” 2. “Disabled by a Malfunction token.” The same rule labels Cabins “passive,” establishing it is not a paid Room Action."},
{"id":"Q42","requested_parts":["Whether Command Center may select a distant Room and choose individual Doors to open or close.","Whether it can close a Destroyed Door."],"answer":"1. Yes. Choose any Room, including a distant one, then Open or Close any of the Doors in its connected Corridors, choosing which ones. 2. It cannot close a Destroyed Door; only the Plasma Torch can close a Destroyed Door.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Command Center — choose any 1 Room and Open/Close any of the Doors in its connected Corridors (your choice which).” 2. “Destroyed = lying token: movement allowed; only the Plasma Torch Item can Close a Destroyed Door. Other Door-closing effects cannot Close it.”"},
{"id":"Q43","requested_parts":["What Engine Control Room can check or change.","How an Engine Room Malfunction affects the check."],"answer":"1. Engine Control Room can check the status of all 3 Engines, but cannot change Engine status there. 2. The check works even if an Engine Room has a Malfunction marker.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Engine Control Room — check the status of all 3 Engines (works even if an Engine Room has a Malfunction marker; you cannot CHANGE Engine status here).” 2. The same complete Room rule explicitly says the check “works even if an Engine Room has a Malfunction marker.”"},
{"id":"Q44","requested_parts":["What Hatch Control System changes.","How Monitoring Room inspects a location, including explored status, components seen, secrecy, and what happens afterward."],"answer":"1. Hatch Control System flips 1 Escape Pod token to Locked or Unlocked. 2. Monitoring Room secretly looks at 1 unexplored Room tile and its Exploration token, then replaces them; the player may lie about what they saw.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Hatch Control System — flip 1 Escape Pod token to Locked or Unlocked.” 2. “Monitoring Room — secretly look at any 1 unexplored Room tile and its Exploration token, then replace them (you may lie about what you saw).”"},
{"id":"Q45","requested_parts":["Whether entry requires an Action to acquire Slime.","Whether Room Covered With Slime has an Item Counter or can receive a Malfunction.","Whether Slime can stack.","Whether Shower extinguishes Fire."],"answer":"1. No Action is needed: entering Room Covered With Slime automatically gives the Character a Slime marker before its Exploration token resolves. 2. It has no Item count and Malfunction markers can never be placed there. 3. Slime is limited to 1 marker on a Character; further Slime does nothing. 4. No. Fire can exist in Shower Room and showering does not remove it.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Room Covered With Slime — no Action: entering automatically gives you a Slime marker before resolving its Exploration token, so a Silence token resolves as Danger (no Item count is set here; Malfunction markers can never be placed here; no Search Action).” 2. The same sentence states “no Item count is set here” and “Malfunction markers can never be placed here.” 3. “Slime marker: while on your board, every ‘Silence’ result (Noise roll or Exploration token) becomes ‘Danger.’ Max 1 (further Slime does nothing). Removed by the Clothes Item or the Shower Room Action.” 4. “Shower Room — discard your Slime marker; you may also Scan all Contamination cards in your HAND and remove all non-Infected ones (Infected → Larva, as in the Canteen). Fire can exist here normally and is NOT removed by showering.”"},
{"id":"Q46","requested_parts":["What can be checked or changed in Cockpit and who sees Coordinate information.","What Destination refers to.","Restrictions on setting/changing Destination when an Intruder is present or after someone hibernates."],"answer":"1. Check Coordinates means privately look at the Coordinates card; you may lie to others, then replace it and remember it. Set Destination moves the Destination marker. 2. The marker selects one of the four destination entries on the Coordinates card, and the corresponding entry is the ship's selected destination. 3. You cannot Set Destination with an Intruder in the Cockpit, or change Destination once anyone has hibernated. Also, Cockpit Room Actions are unavailable while in Combat, such as when an Intruder is present.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Cockpit — Check Coordinates (secretly look at the Coordinates card; you may lie; replace it and remember it) OR Set Destination (move the Destination marker).” 2. “The marker selects one of the four destination entries on the Coordinates card: read the entry corresponding to its track space when checking where the ship will jump.” 3. “You can never Set Destination with an Intruder in the Cockpit, or change Destination once anyone is hibernating.” The common Room Action rule states: “You cannot perform a Room Action while in Combat or in a Room with a Malfunction marker.”"},
{"id":"Q47","requested_parts":["How Engine status checking differs from Repair/Break and what information may be kept secret.","What must be announced when repairing/breaking.","Whether a prior check or functioning Engine Room is required."],"answer":"1. Checking an Engine secretly reveals only the top token; the player may lie about what they saw. Repair/Break secretly examines both Engine tokens and restacks them in any order; you need not disclose whether you repaired it. 2. You must announce if you changed the order of the two tokens. 3. No prior check is needed. A Malfunction does not prevent Repair/Break; it works even with a Malfunction marker. The rules place Repair/Break in an Engine Room, while Engine Control Room can check all three engines but cannot change their status.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Engines #1–#3 — Check the Engine (secretly look at the TOP token; you may lie). Repair/Break Engine (via a Repairs Action or Tools Item): secretly examine both tokens and restack them in any order — you needn't say whether you repaired it, but you MUST say if you changed the order.” 2. The complete Repair/Break sentence in part 1 states: “you MUST say if you changed the order.” 3. “Works even with a Malfunction marker and without checking first.” Engine Control Room: “check the status of all 3 Engines (works even if an Engine Room has a Malfunction marker; you cannot CHANGE Engine status here).”"},
{"id":"Q48","requested_parts":["When hibernation is legal and the cost/roll and failure condition.","What leaves play on success.","Which later ship actions are forbidden after a Character hibernates.","Whether a later Hibernatorium Malfunction wakes or harms sleepers."],"answer":"1. Hibernation is possible only when Time is on a blue space, the Character is in the Hibernatorium, and there is no Intruder or Malfunction there. Pay 2 Action cards and roll Noise; an Intruder reaching or appearing in the Hibernatorium makes the attempt fail. 2. On success, remove the Character miniature; the Character stops participating until end checks. 3. Once anyone hibernates, no Character may change Destination or initiate Self-Destruct (Coolant Leak Event is an exception for starting Self-Destruct). 4. No; a later Hibernatorium Malfunction does not wake or harm sleepers.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Hibernatorium — Try to hibernate: pay 2 Action cards while present, with Time on a blue space, no Intruder and no Malfunction in the Room. Roll Noise; an Intruder reaching/appearing in the Room makes the attempt fail.” 2. “Otherwise remove your miniature and stop participating until the end checks.” 3. “Once anyone hibernates, no one may change Destination or initiate Self-Destruct as a Character; Coolant Leak Event remains an exception.” 4. “A later Hibernatorium Malfunction does not wake/harm sleepers.”"},
{"id":"Q49","requested_parts":["What happens when Time reaches its final red space.","What happens when the last active Character dies, hibernates, or launches, including active Self-Destruct resolution.","Whether Intruders die in a normal hyperspace jump."],"answer":"1. When the Time marker reaches the final red space, the ship jumps to hyperspace; every Character aboard who is not hibernating dies from g-force. 2. When the last alive, non-hibernating Character aboard dies, hibernates, or launches in a Pod, the game ends: move an active Self-Destruct marker to its final red space, or otherwise move the Time marker to its final red space, and resolve the resulting effects. 3. Intruders are unharmed by a normal hyperspace jump; they die if the ship explodes from Self-Destruct or hull-integrity loss.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “The Time marker reaches the final red space of the Time Track — the ship jumps to hyperspace immediately; every Character on board who is not hibernating dies from g-force. Intruders are unharmed by the hyperjump (so objectives like killing the Queen/a Breeder or destroying the Nest fail if not already done).” 2. “The last alive, non-hibernating Character on board dies, hibernates, or launches in an Escape Pod. Then: if the Self-Destruct sequence was active, move its marker to its final red space; if not, move the Time marker to its final red space — and resolve those effects as above.” 3. The Time marker rule quoted in part 1 says Intruders are unharmed by hyperjump. Conversely, the ship-explosion rule states: “The Self-Destruct marker reaches the final red (‘skull’) space of its Track, OR you are instructed to place a 9th Fire marker, OR a 9th Malfunction marker — the ship explodes / loses hull integrity: ALL Characters on board (awake and hibernated) die, and everything else aboard dies too, including Intruders (relevant to some Objectives).”"},
{"id":"Q50","requested_parts":["Whether Characters can directly attack one another.","Whether Actions can indirectly harm other crew and source-supported examples."],"answer":"1. No. Characters cannot directly attack other Characters because of anti-aggression implants. 2. Yes, they can harm crew indirectly; examples include locking someone in a burning Room, activating Self-Destruct with people aboard, and throwing a Grenade into a Room with other crew.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Characters can never directly attack other Characters (anti-aggression implants) — but they may harm them indirectly: locking someone in a burning Room, activating Self-Destruct with people aboard, throwing a Grenade into a Room with other crew, etc.” 2. The same complete sentence gives the indirect examples: “locking someone in a burning Room, activating Self-Destruct with people aboard, throwing a Grenade into a Room with other crew, etc.”"}
]`;
