const raw = String.raw`[
{"id":"Q51","requested_parts":["The explicit overflow procedure when an Adult must be placed and all eight models are on board.","What happens if all eight Adults are in Combat.","What happens when an Intruder moves into an unexplored Room."],"answer":"1. Remove all Adult models not in Combat, returning their tokens to the bag if possible, then place an Adult in the Encounter Room. 2. If all eight Adults are in Combat, the summary says the source does not explain how to supply the required model; an authoritative clarification is needed. 3. Leave the Intruder miniature in the unexplored Room on the board; the Room tile and Exploration token stay face down. Entering an unexplored Room does not remove the Intruder.","support":"partial","evidence":"1. “Adult overflow: if you must place an Adult Intruder but all 8 models are on the board, the Adults retreat — remove all Adult models NOT in Combat and return their tokens to the bag (if possible), then place an Adult model in the Encounter Room.” 2. “If all 8 Adults are in Combat, this source does not explain how to supply the required model; an authoritative clarification is needed.” 3. “Intruders in unexplored Rooms: leave the Intruder miniature in that Room, on the board. The Room tile and its Exploration token both stay face down. Entering an unexplored Room does not remove the Intruder; entering a Technical Corridor uses the separate removal rule.”"},
{"id":"Q52","requested_parts":["Target-size restrictions for the small curled symbol when Shooting.","Target-size restrictions for the larger curved symbol when Shooting.","What Melee does on those symbols against an ineligible target."],"answer":"1. On the small curled symbol, Shooting deals 1 Injury to a Larva or Creeper; it misses against other Intruder types. 2. On the larger curved symbol, Shooting deals 1 Injury to a Larva, Creeper, or Adult; it misses against a Breeder or Queen. 3. Melee uses the same target restrictions and deals 1 Injury when the target is eligible; against an ineligible target, it misses and the attacker suffers 1 Serious Wound.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. Combat-die table: “Small curled Intruder symbol | 1 Injury to Larva/Creeper; miss against other types.” 2. “Larger curved Intruder symbol | 1 Injury to Larva/Creeper/Adult; miss against Breeder/Queen.” 3. For each symbol the Melee column states “Same target restriction and 1 Injury; ineligible target means miss and 1 Serious Wound.”"},
{"id":"Q53","requested_parts":["Whether a four-card draw is required when there is no Larva and all Contamination scans are non-Infected.","Whether scanned non-Infected cards are removed by this endgame procedure.","Whether a previously launched Pod passenger is included in the final Objective check."],"answer":"1. No. With no Larva and no Infected card found among the scans from Action deck, hand, and discard, there is no four-card draw. 2. No cards are removed merely because they scanned non-Infected in this endgame procedure. 3. Launching earlier does not avoid the Objective check: Pod passengers remain in the endgame check and surviving Characters reveal Objectives and win only if fulfilled.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Contamination, for every surviving Character, including launched Pod passengers: No Larva already on board: Scan every Contamination card from Action deck, hand and discard pile. If none is Infected, there is no four-card draw.” 2. “This endgame procedure does not remove cards merely because they scanned non-Infected.” 3. The Contamination rule quoted in part 1 says it applies to “every surviving Character, including launched Pod passengers.” The Objective step states: “Objectives: each still-living Character reveals their chosen Objective and wins only if all requirements are fulfilled. Merely surviving is insufficient.”"},
{"id":"Q54","requested_parts":["Antidote's two component symbols.","Taser's two component symbols.","Flamethrower's two component symbols.","Molotov Cocktail's two component symbols."],"answer":"1. Antidote: flame + medical cross. 2. Taser: battery/lightning + screwdriver/tool. 3. Flamethrower: flame + screwdriver/tool. 4. Molotov Cocktail: flame + folded cloth/fabric. These are component symbols; discard Item cards showing the matching blue icons.","support":"supported","evidence":"1–4. “The four Crafted Items and their component symbols (manual page 23) are: Antidote = flame + medical cross; Taser = battery/lightning + screwdriver/tool; Flamethrower = flame + screwdriver/tool; Molotov Cocktail = flame + folded cloth/fabric. These are component symbols: discard two Items showing the matching blue icons, rather than assuming a particular Item name.”"},
{"id":"Q55","requested_parts":["What defines being in Combat.","How In Combat Only and Out of Combat Only restrictions apply.","Whether unmarked Actions or Items may be used in either situation.","Whether Eggs cause Combat."],"answer":"1. A Character is in Combat whenever an Intruder is in their Room. 2. In Combat Only actions may be used only while an Intruder is in the Room; Out of Combat Only actions may be used only while there is no Intruder in the Room. 3. An Action or Item Action with no Combat marking may be used in either situation, subject to its own requirements; Basic and Room Actions retain their separate limits. 4. Eggs do not count as Intruders and therefore do not cause Combat.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “In Combat / Out of Combat: you are in Combat whenever an Intruder is in your Room. Eggs do not count as Intruders.” 2. Table: “In Combat only | You may use it only while an Intruder is in your Room.” “Out of Combat only | You may use it only while no Intruder is in your Room.” 3. “No Combat marking on a card or Item Action | You may use it in either situation, subject to its own requirements. Basic and Room Actions retain the separate limits below.” 4. The first sentence in part 1 explicitly states Eggs do not count as Intruders."},
{"id":"Q56","requested_parts":["Whether you must personally kill Player X for an Objective requiring that they not survive.","Whether hibernation or escaping counts as death for that Objective.","How the only-survivor Objective differs.","What your own Character must do regardless."],"answer":"1. No. Player X's Character must be dead at game's end, but you do not have to kill them with your own Actions. 2. Hibernating or escaping means that Character survived, so neither fulfills the requirement that they not survive. 3. The only-survivor Objective instead requires that no other Character survive. 4. Regardless of Objective, your Character must survive by Escape Pod or hibernation.","support":"supported","evidence":"1–2. “Player X's Character cannot survive — that Character must simply be dead at game's end (not hibernated, not escaped); you don't have to kill them with your own Actions.” 3. “‘Your Character is the only survivor’ — no other Character may survive.” 4. “Regardless of Objective, your Character must survive (Escape Pod or hibernation).”"},
{"id":"Q57","requested_parts":["What to do when a Door must be placed but the Door pool is empty.","How Open, Closed, and Destroyed states are represented.","Whether multiple Door tokens may occupy one Corridor or Doors may be placed in Technical Corridors."],"answer":"1. Move any Door token from elsewhere on the board. 2. Open means no token; Closed is a standing token; Destroyed is a lying token. 3. No. Door tokens have a maximum of 1 per Corridor and can never be placed in Technical Corridors.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “If the Door pool is empty, move any Door token from elsewhere on the board.” 2. “Open = no token (all Doors start Open). Closed = standing token: blocks Character and Intruder movement (and Grenade throws).” “Destroyed = lying token: movement allowed; only the Plasma Torch Item can Close a Destroyed Door.” 3. “Door tokens: live only in Corridors, max 1 per Corridor.” “Door tokens can never be placed in Technical Corridors.”"},
{"id":"Q58","requested_parts":["What happens to every Pod when the first Character dies.","Whether Pods may later be Locked again."],"answer":"1. All Escape Pods automatically become Unlocked; flip their tokens. 2. Yes. For the rest of the game, Pods may be Locked or Unlocked normally, including being Locked again.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “First dead Character: all Escape Pods are automatically Unlocked (flip tokens).” 2. “For the rest of the game they may be Locked/Unlocked normally (e.g., via the Hatch Control System).”"},
{"id":"Q59","requested_parts":["What Storage's Item Room Action lets you draw and keep and where the rejected card goes.","Common Room Action cost and restrictions."],"answer":"1. Draw 2 cards from a chosen Red, Yellow, or Green Item deck; keep 1 and put the other face down on the bottom of that deck. 2. Room Actions cost 2 Action cards and require the Character to be in that Room, outside Combat, and in a Room without a Malfunction marker.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Storage — draw 2 cards from a chosen Item deck (Red/Yellow/Green), keep 1, other to the bottom of that deck.” The discard-routing rule states for the Search/Storage exception: “Search and Storage keep one of two Item cards and return the other face down to the bottom of the chosen deck.” 2. “Most Rooms grant a specific Action (see Player Reference). Cost: 2 Action cards. Room Actions are local: your Character must be in the Room whose Action you use, unless an explicit special ability permits otherwise. You cannot perform a Room Action while in Combat or in a Room with a Malfunction marker.”"},
{"id":"Q60","requested_parts":["How to take an Egg still represented on the Intruder board, its Action cost, and what follows immediately.","How this differs from picking up a dropped Egg in the current Room."],"answer":"1. While in the Nest, perform its Take one Egg Room Action, paying 2 Action cards; take 1 Egg token from the Intruder board and then roll Noise. 2. A previously dropped Egg in your current Room is picked up with the Pick Up Heavy Object Basic Action for 1 Action card; this pickup has no Noise roll and does not restore the Nest's Egg total.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Still represented on the Intruder board | In the Nest, use its Take one Egg Room Action: 2 Action cards | Take 1 Egg from the Intruder board, then roll Noise.” 2. “Previously dropped into your current Room | Pick Up Heavy Object Basic Action: 1 Action card | Pick up the dropped Egg; this pickup itself has no Noise roll. It does not restore the Nest's Egg total.”"}
]`;
