{"id":"Q81","requested_parts":["What kind of Action activates a Quest Item.","Whether activation is permitted in Combat.","Whether a Room Malfunction prevents activation."],"answer":"1. Activate a Quest Item with the cost-1 Basic Action printed on its card; activation flips the Quest card into its active Item. 2. No. Activation is forbidden in Combat. 3. A Room Malfunction does not prevent this activation.","support":"supported","evidence":"“Quest activation is a Basic Action printed on its card, forbidden in Combat but allowed in a Malfunctioning Room (Actions 3; Items 10).” “Each activation below is a cost-1 Basic Action, out of Combat. It flips the Quest card into its active Item; a Room Malfunction does not prevent this activation.”"},
{"id":"Q82","requested_parts":["Which Basic Actions are permitted in Combat.","Whether Trade, Craft, and Pick Up Heavy Object are permitted in Combat.","What restrictions apply to permitted Action-card or Item Actions."],"answer":"1. The Basic Actions allowed in Combat are Shoot, Melee, and Basic Movement as Escape. 2. No: Trade, Craft, and Pick Up Heavy Object are not allowed in Combat. 3. Action-card and Item Actions follow their own Combat markings and requirements: In Combat Only requires an Intruder in your Room, Out of Combat Only requires none, and unmarked actions can be used in either situation subject to their requirements. Room Actions remain forbidden in Combat.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Basic Actions in Combat are limited to Shoot, Melee and Basic Movement as Escape.” 2. The same rule states “no Trade, Craft, Pick Up Heavy Object or Quest Item activation.” 3. “In Combat only | You may use it only while an Intruder is in your Room.” “Out of Combat only | You may use it only while no Intruder is in your Room.” “No Combat marking on a card or Item Action | You may use it in either situation, subject to its own requirements. Basic and Room Actions retain the separate limits below.” “Room Actions remain forbidden (FAQ Actions 3–5).”"},
{"id":"Q83","requested_parts":["Whether Soldier may use Covering Fire for themselves alone.","How the two Characters choose direction and how many Noise rolls occur.","Whose hand is used for the Surprise Attack comparison."],"answer":"1. Yes. Soldier may use Covering Fire for themselves alone. 2. If both Characters move, each goes in the direction of their choice and they make one Noise roll. 3. If the roll triggers an Encounter, check Surprise against Soldier.","support":"supported","evidence":"“Covering Fire: Soldier may use it for themselves alone. Both moving Characters go in the direction of their choice, then perform one Noise roll; an Encounter checks Surprise against Soldier (Actions 7).”"},
{"id":"Q84","requested_parts":["How Full Auto uses and consumes Ammo.","How Full Auto calculates damage."],"answer":"1. Discard all Ammo from the Assault Rifle and perform one Shoot Action. 2. Damage adds the Shoot result, half the discarded Ammo rounded down, and the Assault Rifle's +1 bonus.","support":"supported","evidence":"“Soldier's Full Auto | 0; In Combat only | Discard all Ammo from your Assault Rifle and perform a Shoot Action; the Ammo discarded determines the additional Injuries as clarified under the FAQ below.” “Full Auto: remove all Ammo, perform one Shoot Action; damage adds the Shoot result, half discarded Ammo rounded down, and the Assault Rifle's +1 bonus (Actions 8).”"},
{"id":"Q85","requested_parts":["When Nerves of Steel may be chosen relative to seeing the Attack card.","Whether it can protect Soldier when another Character triggered a Surprise Attack and who benefits."],"answer":"1. You may see the Intruder Attack card before choosing Nerves of Steel. 2. Yes, it may protect Soldier when another Character triggered the Surprise Attack, but only Soldier benefits; the other Character(s) still suffer applicable effects such as Frenzy.","support":"supported","evidence":"“Nerves of Steel: you may see the Intruder Attack card before choosing it. It can protect Soldier when another Character triggered a Surprise Attack, but only Soldier benefits; other Characters still take effects such as Frenzy (Actions 9–10).”"},
{"id":"Q86","requested_parts":["What happens to automatic Danger/Silence Exploration results under Careful Movement.","What happens to those results under Reconnaissance."],"answer":"1. Careful Movement replaces the automatic Silence/Danger Exploration result with its chosen Noise-marker placement; do not also apply the token's automatic result. 2. Reconnaissance cancels those automatic results.","support":"supported","evidence":"“Careful Movement (cost 2) — follows normal Movement restrictions, including Closed Doors blocking passage; but instead of a Noise roll after moving, place a Noise marker in a chosen Corridor connected to the Room you enter. You cannot Carefully Move if all Corridors connected to the destination already hold Noise markers, and you cannot use it while in Combat. The FAQ clarifies that Careful Movement also replaces automatic Silence/Danger Exploration results with its chosen Noise placement; Reconnaissance cancels those automatic results too.”"},
{"id":"Q87","requested_parts":["Whether Captain can use Order while personally in Combat.","Whether Intercom can make another Character Move out of Combat and what rules that movement follows."],"answer":"1. No. Captain cannot use Order while personally in Combat. 2. Yes. Intercom may make someone else Move out of Combat, but that Character follows the normal Escape rules, including Intruder Attacks before moving.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Captain cannot use Order while personally in Combat.” 2. “Intercom may make someone else Move out of Combat, applying normal Escape.” “Character Escape: to leave a Room containing Intruders, perform a Movement Action (cost 1) to a neighboring Room (explored or unexplored). BEFORE moving, resolve an Intruder Attack — one separate Attack per Intruder in the Room, starting with the larger ones (Queen, Breeder, Adult, Larva; FAQ Rules 5).”"},
{"id":"Q88","requested_parts":["Which Characters may draw an Action card when Captain uses Motivation with active Intercom."],"answer":"1. All Characters in Rooms with a Computer, and all Characters in the Captain's Room, are affected and may draw the Action card.","support":"supported","evidence":"“Motivation with Intercom affects all Characters in Rooms with a Computer as well as all Characters in Captain's Room (Actions 12–13).”"},
{"id":"Q89","requested_parts":["Where a Door from an Exploration token would go when entering by Technical Corridors movement.","Whether the Door is instead placed somewhere else."],"answer":"1. No Door is placed. Entering by the Technical Corridors Action or Technical Corridor Plans ignores a Door Exploration token because the entry Corridor cannot be determined; therefore it has no resolvable Corridor destination.","support":"supported","evidence":"“Entering via Technical Corridors Action or Technical Corridor Plans ignores a Door Exploration token because the entry Corridor cannot be determined (Actions 14).”"},
{"id":"Q90","requested_parts":["Whether a remote Captain Order through Intercom can be Interrupted from outside the Captain's Room.","Whether passing prevents playing an Interruption card."],"answer":"1. No. Interruption requires being in the same Room as the Character performing the interrupted Action, so a remote Order via Intercom cannot be Interrupted from elsewhere. 2. Passing does not prevent Interruption; it remains permitted after passing subject to its usual card requirements.","support":"supported","evidence":"1. “Interruption may still be played after passing, but it requires being in the same Room as the Character performing the interrupted Action; a remote Order via Intercom cannot be Interrupted from elsewhere (Rules 21; Actions 17).” 2. “You may still play Interruption after passing, subject to its usual card requirements (FAQ Rules 21). This is an explicit exception to the normal-Action prohibition.”"}
]`;
